# Lesson 3, new exercise: give up a club early, while you still hold winners in the other suits.
title: Lose a club early
id: L03-05
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2, 3.4, 3.5
student: S
board: 31
dealer: S
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K62    854    73     AK543
  E  43     QJT9   KQT9   JT8
  S  A85    AK3    AJ64   762
  W  QJT97  762    852    Q9

auction:
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 16 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 7-9
  spades: 2 = The ace and king. Dummy's king stops the spades a second time.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 1 = The ace.
  clubs: 2-4 = Two on top. Two more if you give up a club and they split 3-2.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Where can two more come from? What will you have to give up to get them?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S SA
      note: Seven winners on top: two spades, two hearts, one diamond and two clubs. Your eight clubs can give two more, but only after you lose one club trick. Win with the ace and keep dummy's ♠K to stop the spades again.
  2   S C2  W C9  N C3*  E C8
      note: Start on clubs, and let the opponents win the first round. You have to lose a club anyway, so lose it now.
      C3 key: Duck. You must lose a club sooner or later, and losing it now keeps the ace and king in dummy, with a small club left in your hand to reach them.
      C3 not CA: That works here, because dummy has the ♠K as a way back. But did you think about ducking first? You must lose a club anyway, and ducking keeps control of the suit.
      C3 not CK: That works here, because dummy has the ♠K as a way back. But did you think about ducking first? You must lose a club anyway, and ducking keeps control of the suit.
  3   W SJ  N SK  E S4  S S5
      note: The ♠K stops the spades a second time, and puts you in dummy.
  4   N CA  E CT  S C6  W CQ
      note: Now the ace and king of clubs.
  5   N CK  E CJ  S C7  W H2
      note: Both opponents followed twice. Dummy's last two clubs are winners.
  6   N C5  E D9  S D4  W S7
      note: Keep cashing dummy's clubs.
  7   N C4  E DT  S D6  W S9
      note: Dummy's last club winner.
  8   N H4  E HT  S HA  W H6
      note: Back to your hand for the rest of your winners.
  9   S HK  W H7  N H5  E HJ
      note: The ♥K.
  10  S DA  W D2  N D3  E DQ
      note: That's nine tricks. The rest belong to the defense.
  11  S DJ  W D5  N D7  E DK
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  12  E HQ  S H3  W D8  N H8
  13  E H9  S S8  W ST  N S6
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count seven winners on top, then get two more from the clubs by
  giving up a club trick early.

  Ducking the first club was the key. You had to lose a club anyway, and
  losing it first kept control: dummy still had the ace and king, and you
  still had a small club to lead to them. Here the ♠K was another way to
  dummy, but without it, ducking first is the only way to take four club
  tricks. And when you lost the club, you still held a stopper in every
  suit, so nothing the defense did could hurt you.
